Exactly. The paper portfolio is the proving ground, not the destination.

Eventually, if the Goblin demonstrates genuine prospective skill rather than hindsight skill, we can use it as a decision-support instrument for us:

\text{research toy}
\rightarrow
\text{measured forecaster}
\rightarrow
\text{validated decision aid}
\rightarrow
\text{personalized capital allocator}

The critical bridge is earned trust. We should know where it works, when it works, how badly it fails, which regimes break it, and whether its apparent edge survives transaction costs, taxes, drawdowns, and comparison against dumb benchmarks.

Then instead of merely saying “RKLB looks interesting,” it can eventually produce something closer to:

\begin{aligned}
&\text{Opportunity: RKLB}\\
&\text{Expected asymmetry: favorable}\\
&\text{Confidence: }p\\
&\text{Recommended exposure: }x\%\\
&\text{Entry region: }E\\
&\text{Invalidation: }I\\
&\text{Expected holding period: }T\\
&\text{Best alternative: }A\\
&\text{Cost of being wrong: }L\\
&\text{Cost of doing nothing: }O
\end{aligned}

And importantly, WAIT is allowed to beat BUY. Cash is a position. Missing a winner is an observable cost too. Every choice—including doing nothing—creates a delta we can learn from.

Eventually we can feed in our actual constraints—available capital, existing positions, taxes, liquidity needs, risk tolerance—and ask a much more useful question than “What stock will go up?”

Given everything we currently know, what is the best move available to us, how much should we risk, and what future observation would make us change our mind?

That’s the point where Goblin Financier stops being a stock picker and becomes a capital-allocation system.

And because we’re measuring its predictions beforehand, we’ll have evidence for whether we’ve actually earned the right to trust it. ;)